{"page_content": "ACME/Flame Design Final Report (2023) \n \n6 April 2023 \n70 \n \n \ngraph shows the largest flame with the highest flow rate (red) quenched after less than 10 seconds of \noscillating; whereas, the smallest flame with half the flow rate (blue) oscillated for almost 40 seconds \nbefore quenching. \n \nWeek 15: Jan. 21-27 \nOn GMT 024 and 026, Pyotr Dubrov replaced the gas bottles at CIR Manifold #2 and CIR Manifold #4 to \nsupport continuation of ACME Flame Design test points. On GMT 024, we ignited 20 inverse flames for \nFlame Design. We performed these tests with ethylene fuel in a range of oxidizer concentrations and flow \nrates from the burner. These tests were performed at two different chamber pressures. While pressure \nhas a secondary effect on flames compared to the concentration of the reactants, its effect is still \nimportant and noticeable. \n \n \nFigure 1. Effect of Pressure on Flame Design Inverse Flames \n \nThis composite image shows the effect of chamber pressure on inverse flames. Although the pressure \ndidn't greatly affect the size of the flames, it still had a significant effect. The flame at lower pressure is \nmuch brighter because at higher pressures light-emitting molecules are more likely to be extinguished \ndue to molecular collisions. Also, the low-pressure flame shows subtle signs of soot formation. At the top \nof the flame, the blue edge is tinted with yellow due to soot, which also creates a haze just above the \nflame. Inverse flames are inside out, where the soot is formed outside of the flame when the hot fuel \ncombines with itself in the absence of oxygen (which is inside the flame). The porous spherical burner is \nilluminated in red from the illumination package we sometimes use during imaging. \n \nWeek 16: Jan. 28 \u2013 Feb. 3 \nOn GMT 027, we ignited 16 inverse flames for ACME Flame Design. We performed these tests in a \ncombustion chamber filled with an ethylene/nitrogen mixture to a pressure of 3 